# estate-triage demo analysis

Analysis date: 2026-05-02
Repository reviewed: estate-triage

## Executive thesis

estate-triage is most compelling when positioned as a deterministic evidence-to-decision engine for pre-sales infrastructure triage. Its value is not that it generates a report. Its value is that it converts messy but ordinary infrastructure, backup, and utilization exports into governed, explainable, privacy-safe work queues that a partner, sales engineer, delivery lead, and customer operations team can use before committing to a deeper assessment.

The core business impact is funnel quality. The tool improves which workloads are discussed first, which data gaps are requested first, which risky records are withheld from customer-facing recommendations, and which motions are pursued with the strongest evidence. That is defensible because the repo has moved beyond a simple CSV scorer into a repeatable operating system for assessment qualification:

- explicit schema contracts
- deterministic identity resolution
- evidence provenance and traceability
- versioned feature and policy engines
- data readiness validation
- privacy-preserving bundles
- ranking modes that expose different commercial motions
- workflow packs that turn findings into action queues
- synthetic real-world data generation for regression, partner training, and coverage testing
- feedback and outcome structures for measuring downstream accuracy and conversion

The strongest commercial wedge is this:

A partner or sales team can ask for ordinary exports, run them locally with no credentials and no external calls, and return within hours with a credible first review queue, a missing-evidence request list, and a defensible explanation for every workload shown or withheld.

That is materially different from a static report generator. It is a repeatable qualification layer that can sit before assessment, migration review, archive review, DR review, and allocation right-sizing review.

## External context used

The business stories below were grounded against public operating guidance and market context, not vendor-specific claims.

1. NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0
   - URL: https://www.nist.gov/publications/nist-cybersecurity-framework-csf-20
   - Why it matters: NIST CSF 2.0 frames cybersecurity risk management as an outcome-oriented discipline that needs clear communication across stakeholders. estate-triage aligns with that pattern by turning infrastructure evidence into explainable outcomes, gaps, and work queues instead of opaque recommendations.

2. NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Contingency Planning Guide for Federal Information Systems
   - URL: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/34/r1/final
   - Why it matters: contingency planning depends on reliable recovery requirements, backup awareness, impact analysis, and recoverability assumptions. estate-triage does not verify backups, but it makes backup metadata visible enough to prioritize DR tier review and follow-up.

3. FinOps Foundation, Usage Optimization capability
   - URL: https://www.finops.org/framework/capabilities/usage-optimization/
   - Why it matters: usage optimization distinguishes between consumption signals and optimization action. estate-triage follows that discipline by separating allocation-only right-sizing from utilization-backed right-sizing and never claiming a dollar saving.

4. Uptime Institute, Annual Outage Analysis 2025
   - URL: https://intelligence.uptimeinstitute.com/index.php/resource/annual-outage-analysis-2025
   - Why it matters: outage preparation and recovery posture remain high-impact executive concerns. estate-triage supports this with a DR tier review queue based on backup recency, restore point count, backup footprint, RPO, and change profile.

5. NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5
   - URL: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final
   - Why it matters: evidence quality, control traceability, and auditability matter when infrastructure assessment findings are used in regulated or security-sensitive environments. estate-triage keeps local evidence, schema versions, and trace data rather than relying on unverifiable narrative claims.

