## Code deep dive

### Deterministic local execution

The CLI does not require credentials, does not call external services, and does not invoke proprietary tools. That keeps the assessment motion low-friction and privacy-aligned. In practical terms, a partner can run it inside a customer-controlled environment or on redacted exports.

The command surface supports:

- direct CSV analysis
- bundle analysis
- validation
- generator profile validation
- real-world data generation
- workflow pack generation
- summary JSON
- assessment JSON
- redaction
- strict mode
- ranking modes

The API layer also matters because it makes the engine embeddable. A repository that only exposes a CLI is a useful utility. A repository that also exposes stable functions and structured artifacts can become core technology inside portals, assessment factories, partner tooling, or internal delivery systems.

### Adapter boundary

The adapter layer has a clear responsibility boundary:

- parse input files
- map columns
- normalize fields
- attach source provenance
- record data quality findings

It does not:

- score workloads
- assign commercial motions
- hide identity conflicts
- call external systems
- change policy semantics

That separation is defensible. It means future adapters can support more export shapes without changing how scores are produced. It also means partners can contribute mappers while the core policy and evidence contracts remain stable.

### Identity resolution

Identity is one of the highest-value parts of the repo. The code prefers UUID-level matches, falls back to normalized names, and exposes conflicts rather than pretending ambiguous records are safe.

Identity states include:

- RESOLVED
- PROBABLE
- CONFLICTED
- DUPLICATE_CANDIDATES
- UNMATCHED

The generated run found:

- 88 UUID matches
- 8 name matches
- 13 unmatched inventory rows
- 3 conflicted records
- 3 duplicate candidate groups

This directly supports business credibility. In real assessments, a wrong join can create a false recommendation, embarrass the partner, and damage trust. estate-triage protects against that by making identity uncertainty first-class evidence.

### Feature registry

The feature layer converts raw fields into explicit, testable signals:

- workload key
- backup-to-used ratio
- change rate percent
- recent backup
- stale backup
- powered on/off
- high allocated CPU
- high allocated memory
- large backup footprint
- low change rate
- storage ratios
- snapshot risk
- utilization-backed CPU and memory signals
- stale inventory
- RPO and restore point signals
- data quality risk

The business value is in the honesty of these features. Allocation is not treated as utilization. Backup size is not treated as business criticality. Powered off is not treated as unused. Low change rate is not treated as a retirement recommendation. That distinction makes the outputs credible for technical buyers.

### Policy engine

The policy pack is versioned and deterministic. The default pack uses simple, explainable rules for:

- MIGRATION_REVIEW
- ARCHIVE_REVIEW
- RIGHTSIZING_REVIEW
- DR_TIER_REVIEW

The policy engine is commercially important because it can become the governed decision layer. A partner organization can standardize thresholds, approval metadata, motion labels, blocking flags, and required evidence. That turns tribal sales-engineering judgment into a repeatable process while still preserving human review.

### Data readiness validation

The validation layer reads files before analysis and reports:

- readiness score
- readiness grade
- recognized rows
- recognized fields
- missing recommended fields
- field profiles
- parse warnings
- mapping confidence
- matchability forecast
- data request checklist

In the generated run:

- utilization input achieved readiness grade A with no findings
- inventory input intentionally produced readiness grade F because it contained contradictory and out-of-range records
- backup input produced readiness grade F because of ambiguous dates and missing/blank mapped fields

That is not a failure of the generator. It is exactly the point. The tool can distinguish usable evidence from evidence that must be corrected before a customer-facing recommendation is made.

### Ranking modes

The repo supports multiple ranking modes:

- balanced
- global
- per_motion
- confidence_first
- blockers_first
- missing_evidence_first

The generated run showed why this matters. In a global top-50 ranking, migration dominated and archive/right-sizing were buried. In balanced mode, the top 50 contained roughly equal representation across the four motions:

- 13 migration
- 12 archive
- 13 right-sizing
- 12 DR

This is a real commercial insight. Different teams need different lists:

- a migration team wants the strongest migration candidates
- a DR specialist wants backup and recovery tier candidates
- a capacity or FinOps team wants right-sizing candidates
- a customer success or account team may want archive review candidates
- an executive sponsor wants a balanced portfolio of next actions

Ranking mode is therefore not a UI preference. It is a sales-motion control.

### Workflow pack

The workflow pack moves the repo beyond "report generation." It creates:

- executive summary
- discovery plan
- work queues
- evidence packets
- task CSV
- ranking comparison

The generated workflow pack produced:

- 109 evidence packets
- migration: 32 strong candidates, 10 needs-more-data, 11 review candidates, 11 do-not-present
- archive: 12 needs-more-data
- right-sizing: 10 strong candidates, 8 do-not-present
- DR: 12 strong candidates, 3 needs-more-data

This is a major business-value feature. It tells the partner which records are ready to present, which require follow-up, and which should not be shown to the customer yet.

### Privacy and handoff

The repo supports redaction, fingerprints, bundle manifests, and local-only processing. Redaction replaces workload names and hashes workload keys while preserving numeric metrics and reason codes.

That enables privacy-safe collaboration:

- a customer can share redacted output with a partner
- a partner can escalate an evidence packet internally without exposing workload names
- a delivery team can compare fingerprints to detect whether inputs changed
- a governance reviewer can understand what policy version and thresholds were used

Privacy is not just a compliance story here. It is a sales-cycle accelerator because it makes the first data-sharing step easier.

### Feedback and outcome structure

The repo includes feedback/outcome mechanisms with labels such as accepted, rejected, duplicate, wrong motion, missed opportunity, and needs more data. The sample feedback is small, but the mechanism matters.

This is how estate-triage can become better business technology over time without becoming an AI system:

- track accepted recommendations
- track rejected recommendations
- track false positives and false negatives
- track which blockers most often delay customer action
- track which missing fields are most often requested
- track motion conversion after deeper assessment
- compare policy versions against outcomes

That is the path from deterministic tool to operational learning system.

